no sooner had they knelt  than it began  the sound of booing echoing around the  den before the match against derby  county  and it was definitely the millwall fans  they were the only ones allowed in under  new rules brought in this week  to get home supporters back in the  stands for the first time since  march their response to the player's  action against racism  has been widely condemned in a statement  millwall football club said it was  dismayed and saddened by the booing  adding that the club had been working  tirelessly to prepare for the return of  supporters  and what should have been a positive and  exciting occasion  had been completely overshadowed but the  focus of the incident took a new turn  after a government minister spoke about  the politicization of the movements  to end racial discrimination my personal  view is that  black lives matter capital b  l and m is actually a political movement  that is  uh that is different to what most of us  believe in which is  standing up for racial equality but look  at each  individual can take their own choices  about how they  reflect this and i know a number of  people feel quite strongly and have  taken that approach  his comments have been greeted with  disappointment  i was shocked to be honest it just shows  how privileged he is  it shows white privilege that he can  label it as a political movement and  move on from it  i would say personally it's more of a  social movement that is fighting  equality  and racial injustice but him labeling it  a political movement and not addressing  the racism not addressing what the fans  have done just so it shows that we've  got a long way to go  players began to go down on one knee at  premier league and championship games  when matches resumed in june  and the football association has made it  clear it supports players who wish to do  so  condemning the reaction by millwall  spectators  but as we were filming at the club paul  golding the leader of the far  right group britain first turned up  after recording his own video  in support of the booing fans maximizing  the opportunity it's provided  millwall has said it will ensure it  stays at the forefront of football's  anti-discrimination  efforts but the incident has proved an  unsavory reminder  of the overt racism all too common in  football  in times gone by emma birchley sky news
 

